  "cli_result_text": "Submitted 22 files covering the full dependency chain requested.\n\n**Log4j2 side:**\n- `log4j-api/spi/Provider.java`, `LogManager.java`, `util/ProviderUtil.java` \u2014 the Provider SPI that Log4j2's `LogManager` uses to select a logging backend (ServiceLoader-based `META-INF/services/org.apache.logging.log4j.spi.Provider`), including the priority-selection logic (`factories.get(factories.lastKey())` \u2014 highest priority number wins when multiple providers are present).\n- `log4j-core/impl/Log4jProvider.java` (priority 10) + its service file \u2014 binds the Log4j2 API to `log4j-core`'s own `Log4jContextFactory`.\n- `log4j-to-slf4j/SLF4JProvider.java` (priority **15**) + its service file \u2014 binds the Log4j2 API to SLF4J instead, meaning if both `log4j-core` and `log4j-to-slf4j` are on the classpath, SLF4J wins.\n- `log4j-slf4j-impl` and `log4j-slf4j2-impl` poms \u2014 the *other* direction (SLF4J \u2192 Log4j2-core binding); 2.20.0 added the SLF4J 2.x variant.\n- Module poms (`log4j-api`, `log4j-to-slf4j`, `log4j-core`, root `pom.xml`) establishing the module/dependency structure.\n\n**Spring Boot side:**\n- `Log4J2LoggingSystem.java` \u2014 its `Factory.PRESENT` check probes for `org.apache.logging.log4j.core.impl.Log4jContextFactory`, a class that lives in `log4j-core`, **not** `log4j-api` or `log4j-to-slf4j`.\n- `LogbackLoggingSystem.java`, `LoggingSystem.java`, `LoggingSystemFactory.java`, `DelegatingLoggingSystemFactory.java`, and `spring.factories` \u2014 the detection/ordering mechanism (Logback checked first, then Log4j2, then JUL).\n- `spring-boot-starter-logging/build.gradle` (default, pulls `log4j-to-slf4j` + Logback) vs `spring-boot-starter-log4j2/build.gradle` (pulls `log4j-slf4j2-impl` + `log4j-core`) \u2014 showing why Spring Boot's default starter puts `log4j-to-slf4j` on the classpath specifically so any library using the Log4j2 API gets routed to Logback, while explicitly avoiding `log4j-core` so `Log4J2LoggingSystem` detection doesn't fire.",
